        <div class="pastproj-container">
          <li ><h4 class="pastproj-subtitle">A Web-based Digital Twin Framework for Monitoring 
            of Floating Offshore Wind Turbine and Virtual Sensing of Mooring Lines (Phase 1)</h4></li>
          
          <!-- Introduction -->
          <section class="section-content" id="introduction">
              <h5>Introduction</h5>
              <p>
                  Global electricity demand is rising rapidly with electric vehicles and AI data centers. 
                  Offshore wind energy is central to the green energy transition, offering higher capacity factors 
                  and more reliable generation than onshore wind. Floating Offshore Wind Turbines (FOWTs) enable 
                  deployment in deep waters, where strong and consistent winds exist, but introduce complex engineering 
                  challenges in platform dynamics, mooring integrity, and monitoring.
              </p>
              <p>
                  Digital Twin technology creates real-time virtual replicas of physical systems, supporting predictive 
                  maintenance, performance optimization, and structural health monitoring. This project presents a Digital Twin 
                  applied to a scaled 1:40 FOWT model in TCOMS's Ocean Basin, performing cyber-physical testing with live sensor data.
                  You can view a demonstration of the web-based Digital Twin platform 
                  <a href="https://1drv.ms/v/c/d893771471b95466/IQDlO9APNBDETYY39Iz1fNQEAbRHH8MXaoVq-iSZSlSxXxM?e=Mg5T22"><b>here</b></a>!
              </p>
          </section>

          <!-- Objectives -->
          <section class="section-content"id="objectives">
              <h5>Objectives</h5>
              <ul>
                  <li>Monitor FOWT platform motions, turbine dynamics, and mooring loads in real time.</li>
                  <li>Integrate physical testing with live data streams for structural assessment.</li>
                  <li>Provide interactive visualization and analytics for engineers.</li>
                  <li>Enable machine learning-based virtual sensing to estimate mooring line loads.</li>
                  <li>Support both real-time operation and offline simulation for validation and training.</li>
              </ul>
          </section>      

          <!-- Contributions -->
          <section class="section-content"id="contributions">
            <h5>Key Contributions</h5>
            <ul>
              <li>Unified Real-Time and Offline Architecture</li>
              <p>
                Architected and implemented a unified event-driven infrastructure that supports both 
                Ocean Basin real-time experiments and offline dataset replay 
                within a single computational framework, without the needs of modifying core system 
                logic. Established consistent data handling, buffering, throttling, and visualization 
                update mechanisms across operational modes.
              </p>

              <li>End-to-End System Architecture Design and Implementation</li>
              <p>
                Independently conceived, designed, and implemented the complete Digital Twin platform 
                from initial architectural sketches to full system deployment. 
                The implementation includes frontend-backend integration, asynchronous task 
                orchestration, time-series database architecture, containerized 
                deployment, and cross-dashboard data synchronization.
              </p>

              <li>Machine Learning Model Integration and Deployment</li>
              <p>
                Integrated the virtual-sensing machine learning model developed by a collaborator into 
                the Digital Twin infrastructure. Designed and implemented the 
                end-to-end data preprocessing pipeline, including sliding-window buffering to generate 
                fixed-length time-series inputs for the machine learning model. 
                Established asynchronous task execution using Celery workers for model training and 
                inference, and implemented model serialization, 
                persistence, and retraining mechanisms via Docker volumes. Enabled seamless deployment 
                of the machine learning model in both real-time and offline 
                modes under a unified processing pipeline.
              </p>

              <li>Unity-Based 3D Visualization Integration and Enhancement</li>
              <p>
                Integrated and substantially enhanced the Unity-based 3D visualization module originally 
                developed by a collaborator. Implemented real-time WebSocket communication, 
                structural motion and wave visualization and synchronization, fatigue visualization 
                mapping, and interactive UI control mechanisms. 
                Ensured strict temporal alignment between physical sensor measurements and the 
                digital twin representation.
              </p>

              <li>Full Infrastructure Ownership and Technical Responsibility</li>
              <p>
                Served as the sole architect and end-to-end implementer of the Digital Twin platform, 
                holding full technical ownership of system design, integration, 
                deployment, debugging, validation, and performance optimization across all modules. 
                Responsible for maintaining architectural coherence between data 
                acquisition, machine learning services, visualization subsystems, and deployment 
                infrastructure.
              </p>
            </ul>

          </section>

          <!-- System Architecture -->
          <section class="section-content"id="architecture">
              <h5>System Architecture</h5>
              <p>A high-level overview of the Digital Twin architecture is shown in
              Figure 1, comprising three core components: (i) the physical
              assets, equipped with sensors, located in the Ocean Basin; (ii)
              single-directional data ingestion from the physical system to the
              virtual system via a Data Acquisition (DAQ) device; and (iii) a
              Windows-based workstation executing the virtual counterpart.</p>

              <figure class="architecture-figure">
                <img src="/images/architecturepic.png" alt="System Architecture Diagram">
                <figcaption>
                  Figure 1: System Architecture.
                </figcaption>
              </figure>
              
              <p>The system adopts a three-tier architecture comprising
              presentation, application, and data layers, and is deployed using
              Docker containers. The architecture separates concerns across
              these distinct layers that communicate through well-defined
              protocols, this improves scalability and maintainability.</p>

              <h6 class="h6-style">Presentation Layer</h6>
              <p>
                The topmost layer consists of a React-based web application that provides the user interface. 
                It includes four main modules: (i) a WebGL-based 3D visualization component, built with Unity, 
                for real-time and offline rendering of marine platform geometry, mooring lines, and wave surfaces; 
                (ii) a machine learning interface for configuring machine learning model training, managing batch 
                prediction tasks, and monitoring performance dashboards; (iii) a data management module for 
                uploading, organizing, and maintaining time-series data; and (iv) an embedded Grafana dashboard 
                for multi-channel data monitoring. The frontend communicates with the backend through Socket.IO 
                for continuous data streaming, and leverages RESTful APIs to handle data management and file transfer 
                operations.
              </p>

              <h6 class="h6-style">Application Layer</h6>
              <p>
                The middle layer handles all business logic and computational tasks, and comprises two primary 
                components, namely the Flask backend server and a Celery worker pool. The former handles HTTP requests, 
                manages WebSocket connections, and dispatches tasks. Specifically, it exposes RESTful API endpoints 
                for data operations, provides Socket.IO handlers for real-time events, and acts as a Celery client 
                to dispatch asynchronous tasks. The Celery worker pool executes computationally intensive operations 
                through specialized workers: a default worker for general-purpose tasks, two GPU-accelerated workers 
                for real-time training and inference of PyTorch- and MATLAB-based models, and a dedicated wave simulation 
                worker for field reconstruction.
              </p>

              <h6 class="h6-style">Data Layer</h6>
              <p>
                The foundation layer provides persistent storage and messaging infrastructure for the Digital Twin 
                platform. Redis serves as the message broker for Celery task distribution and as a cache for frequently 
                accessed data. TimescaleDB, a PostgreSQL extension optimized for time-series data, stores timestamped 
                measurements, including six platform motion channels (surge, sway, heave, roll, pitch, yaw), mooring 
                line tension loads, additional operational parameters, and virtual sensing predictions of mooring line 
                tensions. File storage volumes contain machine learning model artifacts (e.g., .pt and .mat files), 
                inference and wave field reconstructions packages, system configuration files, and temporary upload 
                staging areas, ensuring data persistence across container restarts.
              </p>

              <h6 class="h6-style">Interfaces for real-time deployment</h6>
              <p>
                The Digital Twin system interfaces with external systems to receive real-time data through: (i) Qualisys 
                Motion Capture which transmits 6-DOF platform motion via TCP/IP; (ii) Mooring line tension forces at the 
                fairlead connection, measured by load cells via TCP/IP; (iii) Turbine rotor speed signals via TCP/IP; and 
                (iv) Wave Probe System which pushes UDP datagrams containing multi-channel wave elevation measurements.
              </p>


          </section>

          <!-- Deployment -->
          <section class="section-content" id="deployment">
            <h5>Deployment of Digital Twin Platform</h5>
            <p>
              The Digital Twin platform implements a unified event-driven architecture that supports both real-time streaming operation 
              and offline replay within a single computational framework. All core functionalities are consistently available 
              in both modes, including model training and retraining, dataset upload and management, and interactive visualization 
              through WebGL-based 3D rendering and Grafana dashboards.
            </p>

            <h6 class="h6-style">Comparison for Real-Time and Offline Modes</h6>

            <p>
              Figure 2 illustrates the configuration for real-time deployment at the Ocean Basin. Testing was conducted 
              across a range of cyber-physical scenarios representing Celtic Sea wind-wave conditions, using a 1:40 scale 
              FOWT model.
              The test setup comprised a semi-submersible floating foundation with a four-column configuration, 
              seven catenary mooring lines with chain-wire-chain composition (each equipped with load cells at the fairlead connections), 
              a six-axis motion capture system via Qualisys, and a multi-channel wave probe array.
              DAQ devices collect real-time measurements from the sensors, while the Local Bridge Application acts as 
              a critical intermediary, pre-processing and transferring data from the DAQ to the Digital Twin platform to ensure 
              compatibility and minimize latency.
              Operating as a standalone process connected to the Ocean Basin instrumentation network, 
              the Local Bridge Application manages multiple simultaneous data streams using different protocols.
              A Socket.IO client transmits the processed data to the backend server for further integration.
            </p>

            <figure class="architecture-figure">
              <img src="images/onlinepic.png" alt="Real-time Deployment">
              <figcaption>Figure 2: Real-time Deployment.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <p>
              Figure 3 illustrates the configuration for offline deployment. Offline operation provides all core 
              functionalities without requiring physical connection to Ocean Basin infrastructure.
            </p>

            <figure>
              <img src="images/offlinepic.png" alt="Offline Deployment">
              <figcaption>Figure 3. Offline Deployment.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <p>
              Historical sensor data can be loaded through a Virtual Data Source Application and processed by the 
              Local Bridge Application, simulating real-time measurement flow. Offline deployment is primarily employed to
              reproduce the motion of the physical model for assessment,
              analyze historical data, as well as train and validate machine
              learning models. Once validated, these models can be deployed
              for real-time operation, enabling live virtual sensing capabilities.
            </p>

            <h6 class="h6-style">Operational Pipeline for Real-Time and Offline Modes</h6>

            <p>
              In real-time mode, the frontend establishes Socket.IO connections to receive live platform motion updates, 
              mooring tension measurements, and reconstructed wave field data.
              In offline mode, recorded datasets are streamed through the same pipeline to ensure consistent 
              processing logic across operational scenarios.
              Incoming sensor data enters a ring buffer maintaining a sliding window of recent measurements. 
              This guarantees that machine learning models receive fixed-length input sequences independent 
              of packet timing irregularities.
              A configurable throttle mechanism regulates processing rates:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>Aligned with sampling frequency in real-time mode</li>
              <li>Adjustable for accelerated playback in offline mode</li>
            </ul>

            <!-- <h6 class="h6-style">Inference Execution</h6> -->
            <p>
              Prediction requests are dispatched asynchronously as Celery tasks routed to appropriate worker queues. 
              Tasks are executed by either Python-based or MATLAB-based workers.
              Inference results are:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>Cached in Redis for low-latency retrieval</li>
              <li>Persisted in TimescaleDB for historical analysis</li>
              <li>Broadcast to clients via Socket.IO and WebSocket</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
              The 3D rendering component uses reactive updates applicable to both modes. Platform motion updates 
              position and orientation continuously, with interpolation ensuring smooth transitions.
              Wave field updates dynamically adjust the animated water surface mesh by vertically displacing vertices 
              according to reconstructed elevations.

              The 3D visualization module, developed in Unity, renders synchronized digital representations 
              of the physical system and surrounding environment:
            </p>

            <ul>
              <li>Platform Geometry: A detailed 3D model includes accurate geometry and realistic joints. 
              The 6-DOF motion is driven directly by time-series sensor data.</li>
              <li>Wave Surface: An animated sea-surface mesh is generated from reconstructed wave field data, 
              synchronizing wave-body interactions with structural motions. </li>
              <li>Control Panel: A customized interactive interface allows toggling of 2D parameter plots and system settings.</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
              Figure 4 presents a WebGL-based virtual replica of a
              1:40 scale floating offshore wind turbine, comprising a
              semi-submersible platform, a three-blade wind turbine, and a
              composite mooring system. In this scenario, no data streaming
              is performed, so both the floating platform and the surrounding
              wave environment remain static.
            </p>

            <figure>
              <img src="images/3dvisualization.png" alt="3D Visualization Dashboard">
              <figcaption>Figure 4. 3D Visualization Dashboard.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <p>
              In Figure 5, wave probe measurements, platform motions, mooring line tensions, 
              and turbine rotor speeds are streamed simultaneously into the 3D visualization 
              module, enabling real-time rendering of both the wave field and the platform's 
              structural response.

              Through the control panel, users can visualize mooring line fatigue levels. 
              Each catenary mooring line is equipped with a color-coded indicator bar 
              (green → yellow → red) that represents fatigue damage accumulation and provides 
              immediate awareness of potential failure risk.

              The interface also allows comparison between measured (blue) and virtual-sensed 
              (red) mooring line tensions to evaluate the accuracy of the machine learning 
              model. The virtual-sensing module estimates mooring line tensions from 
              platform motion data using a Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN). In addition, 
              the anchor depth can be switched between 200 m and 60 m to match the two basin 
              test conditions.
            </p>

            <figure>
              <img src="images/3dfatigue.png" alt="Mooring Fatigue Visualization">
              <figcaption>Figure 5. Measured (blue) and virtual-sensing (red) mooring loads with fatigue levels.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <p>
              Additionally, users can double-click on a specific component
              to zoom in and view detailed information presented in a 2D
              graph. The selected component is highlighted in red, and
              pressing the ESC key restores the previous view and hides the
              detailed display. Figure 6 illustrates the blade hub selected
              and highlighted in red, with its associated information, such as
              rotational speed, displayed in a 2D graph at the bottom left.
            </p>

            <figure>
              <img src="images/3dzoom.png" alt="Zoom-in View">
              <figcaption>Figure 6. Zoom-in View of Blade Hub with 2D Detail Graph.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <h6 class="h6-style">Time-Series Analytics Dashboard</h6>

            <p>
              The time-series analytics dashboard, shown in Figure 7,
              supports both real-time visualization and offline playback
              analysis using Grafana. It presents historical sensor and wave
              probe data stored in the PostgreSQL database, as well as live
              data streamed via WebSocket. Beyond visualization, Grafana
              enables interactive data exploration through customizable
              dashboards, time-range selection, variable filtering, and
              multi-panel comparisons. It also supports alert configuration
              for threshold-based monitoring, annotation of key events, and
              user access control for role-based data management.
            </p>

            <figure>
              <img src="images/2dgrafana.png" alt="Time-series Analytics Dashboard">
              <figcaption>Figure 7. Time-series Analytics Dashboard.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <h6 class="h6-style">Machine Learning Model Training Dashboard</h6>

            <p>
              The Machine Learning Model Training Dashboard, shown
              in Figure 8, supports the development of virtual sensing
              models for estimating mooring line loads at the fairlead.
              The training workflow accommodates both Python-based
              and MATLAB-based machine learning frameworks. For  
              MATLAB-based workflows, the MATLAB Engine API for
              Python is utilized within the Flask backend to execute model
              training and virtual sensing routines.
              To initiate training, users first upload and select the desired
              machine learning model structure. The model structure defines
              the architecture and training configuration, including the number
              of layers, loss function, number of epochs, optimizer settings,
              and other hyperparameters. After specifying the model name,
              selecting the training database, defining the input and output
              variables, and setting the data range for training, the machine
              learning model training process can be initiated.
              When the training process begins, the system checks whether
              an existing model is available for retraining. If a previous model
              exists, it is retrieved from the Docker volume and used as the
              initial state for retraining. Otherwise, a new machine learning
              model is initialized.          
            </p>

            <figure>
              <img src="images/2dmltraining.png" alt="ML Training Dashboard">
              <figcaption>Figure 8. Machine Learning Model Training Dashboard.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <p>
              A dedicated log window displays the model training progress,
              including the current iteration, elapsed time, RMSE, loss values,
              and other relevant metrics, as illustrated in Figure 9. Upon
              completion of the training task, a notification snackbar appears
              to inform the user, and the trained model is saved to the Docker
              volume for future deployment or retraining.
            </p>

            <figure>
              <img src="images/2dmltrainingend.png" alt="Training Progress Log">
              <figcaption>Figure 9. Training Progress Log Window.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <h6 class="h6-style">Machine Learning Model Inference Dashboard</h6>

            <p>
              The Machine Learning Model Inference Dashboard, shown
              in Figure 10, enables performance verification of virtual sensing
              models for estimating mooring line loads. The dashboard
              supports both externally uploaded models and models trained
              through the Machine Learning Model Training Dashboard.
              To initiate inference, users select the desired machine learning
              model, specify the database containing the evaluation dataset,
              define the relevant input and output data columns, and set
              the data range for the inference process. The selected input
              variables are fed into the model to generate predictions, while
              the corresponding measured outputs serve as ground truth for
              performance evaluation and comparison.
              The dashboard provides quantitative assessment of model
              accuracy by comparing predicted and measured values,
              facilitating validation and benchmarking. Inference
              results can also be stored in the database for subsequent analysis
              and visualization.
            </p>

            <figure>
              <img src="images/2dinference.png" alt="ML Inference Dashboard">
              <figcaption>Figure 10. Machine Learning Model Inference Dashboard.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <h6 class="h6-style">Database Management Dashboard</h6>

            <p>
              Figure 11 illustrates the Database Management Dashboard,
              which provides a centralized interface for administering
              PostgreSQL databases. The dashboard displays a list of
              available databases, allowing users to rename databases, mark
              them as favorites for quick access, and export data locally
              in CSV format. It also supports uploading CSV files to
              populate or update database records. In addition, individual
              data entries can be modified or deleted directly through the
              dashboard, providing comprehensive control over the stored
              datasets. Alternatively, users may access and manage the
              databases through Adminer, which offers a web-based interface
              for direct database administration.
            </p>

            <figure>
              <img src="images/database.png" alt="Database Management Dashboard">
              <figcaption>Figure 11. Database Management Dashboard.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <h6 class="h6-style">Validation of 3D Digital Twin Visualization</h6>

            <p>
              The 3D digital twin visualization was validated by comparing
              recorded basin video footage with the rendered digital
              twin animation separately in a side-by-side configuration, as shown in
              Figure 12. Synchronized playback confirmed that the digital
              twin accurately reproduced the platform’s motions. Moreover,
              the visualization offered an intuitive representation of platform
              dynamics that would be difficult to discern from time-series
              plots alone.
            </p>

            <figure>
              <img src="images/compare.png" alt="Basin vs Digital Twin">
              <figcaption>Figure 12. Experimental Basin Video vs. Digital Twin Animation.</figcaption>
            </figure>

          </section>

          <!-- Machine Learning Methodology -->
          <section class="section-content"id="ml">
              <h5>Machine Learning Methodology</h5>
              <p>
                  Mooring line virtual sensing is a sequence-to-sequence regression problem:
              </p>
              <ul>
                  <li><strong>Input:</strong> 6-DOF platform motions, velocities, and accelerations.</li>
                  <li><strong>Output:</strong> Predicted tensions for each mooring line.</li>
                  <li><strong>Model:</strong> Python TCN provides fast, high-accuracy predictions; MATLAB LSTM models remain available for reference.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                  Moving-window inference continuously updates mooring line tensions in real time, enabling synchronized visualization and performance assessment.
              </p>
          </section>
        </div>
      </ul>
